{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T223221Z-1bc3b9c1", "ts": "2026-11-26T22:39:09Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.1-dev", "branch": "fix/git-quotepath", "build": "a05d8b8e5c8c"}, "body": {"base_commit": "a05d8b8e5c8c7efc92893b57ad43a24284b3c738", "base_tree": "db9bc2d67cfd2d4b1616e2bcbeb6127713de3ced", "head_tree": "25c7afed650c783cc9aed0d8436ac5e8d091cea0", "head_commit": "6a273535f0bfa8e4a70198b173022d3f076b3387", "dispatch_commit": "6a273535f0bfa8e4a70198b173022d3f076b3387", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/late_requirements.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py", "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A git name read carries three obligations at every site, and the lint finds a trimming runner only from a hand-kept list", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: the decoder has one home (`gitstate.unquote_git_path` / `git_path_lines`), but running git for names does not. Each reader still has to do three things itself: call an untrimmed runner (`strip=False` on `evidence.run_git`, or `_run_git_text`, never `_git_text`), split on git's newline only, and decode. About 25 sites now repeat `run_git(..., strip=False)` followed by `git_path_lines(out)`, and eight porcelain loops repeat `for line in X.split(\"\\n\"): parsed = parse_porcelain_line(line)`. The two obligations the boundary round added (no trim, split on `\\n`) had to be fixed site by site, which is the core rule's tell: 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class ... fix the class through one owner. Tell: your second fix of one class in two rounds.' The trim obligation is also guarded by enumeration. `tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py` knows a runner trims only if it is in `TRIMMING` or `TRIMMING_IN_FILE`, and `stranded_work.py` gets a special case hardcoded inside `scan()`. Take a new module that adds the common `def _git(p, *a): return evidence.run_git(p, *a)[1]` wrapper (four modules already have one) and reads porcelain or ls-files through it. That reader passes all five lint tests: the call's literals count as a read, the decoder balances it, `_git` is not in the trimming map for that file, and the wrapper forwards `*args`, so the companion test excuses it. Its first unstaged porcelain line (` M x` read as `M x`) is then misparsed, which is exactly the `critic_consolidate.no_judgeable_wip` defect the last round fixed. Close by construction, not with a longer table. Either (a) add one gitstate entry point for name reads (for example `git_names(project_dir, *args) -> list[str] | None` and `git_status_entries(text)`) that runs untrimmed, splits on `\\n` and decodes, and have the lint require name reads to use it or `-z`; or (b) have the lint find trimming wrappers itself: a function that forwards `*args` to a runner without `strip=False`, or calls `.strip()` on its stdout. Then delete `TRIMMING_IN_FILE` and the stranded_work special case.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A git call whose output is read as path names must not be trimmed, must be split on '\\n' only, and must be decoded; any site that meets those three obligations by hand, or any trimming wrapper the lint learns of only by a table entry, is a member.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "tests/test_git_path_read_lint.py::TRIMMING_IN_FILE and the stranded_work special case in scan()"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "porcelain split+parse loops: gitstate.git_has_session_changes, gitstate._get_session_changed_files, coverage._working_tree_paths, critic_mode._get_uncommitted_code_files, critic_consolidate.no_judgeable_wip, gates._session_untracked_paths, risk._review_scope_paths (via risk._git_lines), learnings_compact.undo_refusals"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "run_git(..., strip=False) + git_path_lines pairs across buildplan_refs, change_fragments, coverage, native_review, record_lint, learnings_files, late_requirements, plan_status, prawduct-hook _judge_flakes"}], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 1, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Text-mode subprocess reads rewrite a CR before any -z split or diff parse, so 'git's newline only' and 'reads as it is on disk' miss one byte", "recommendation": "Every git read here runs `subprocess.run(..., encoding=\"utf-8\")`, which is text mode. CPython's communicate() then turns \"\\r\\n\" and a lone \"\\r\" into \"\\n\" before the caller sees stdout. Line reads are unaffected, because git C-quotes a CR (`\\r`), and the generated round-trip test only drives those. A `-z` read is not quoted, so a name holding a CR comes back with a LF in it: `bin/test-reference-verify._changed_files`, which this diff moved onto `-z` with the comment 'reads as it is on disk', and every other `-z` site (`evidence.tree_diff`, `gates.session_work_span`, `coverage._uncommitted_paths`, ...). The macOS custom-icon file `Icon\\r` is the real-world case. It classifies as non-judgeable, so no gate answer changes today. In `record_lint._parse_diff`, `.removesuffix(\"\\r\")` is dead on the production path, and the comment claiming CRLF lines 'keep their \\r that way' describes a mechanism that never runs: a lone CR inside an added record line still splits that line, and the text after it is not linted. No test feeds a CR through either path ('A comment that NAMES a new failure mode owes a test in the same commit'). Scope: class. The members are the readers that decode git output in text mode, where a CR is significant, which means the `-z` name reads and the full-diff reader. It closes by construction: one owner reads git's stdout as bytes, or passes `newline=''`-equivalent handling and decodes with surrogateescape. A longer list of sites does not close it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "ls-tree reader class is pinned with the non-ASCII name only; the plan's two-file fixture names a quote-bearing one too", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's Tests say each reader class (ls-tree, worktree list, git grep -l, diff headers) gets 'the same two-file fixture'. `test_learnings_corpus_texts_at_reads_a_non_ascii_area_file` holds only `r\u00e9seau.md`. Several chunk-01 single readers are also tested with one name: native_review, change_fragments, _pr_diff_is_doc_only, _resolve_artifact, _judge_flakes and no_judgeable_wip, the last with an ASCII name only. Every one of them decodes through the same `unquote_git_path`, and the decoder test exercises both quotings on real git output, so no reader is unpinned in effect. The gap is in the letter of the acceptance criterion. Adding the quote-bearing name to the ls-tree test would make that criterion literally true.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_names.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "learnings_compact.undo_refusals' new rename arm is unasserted", "recommendation": "The refusal now re-renders each porcelain line as `status source -> path` for a rename and as `status path` otherwise. Only the non-rename arm is tested (`?? .claude/rules/learnings/r\u00e9seau.md`). Whether to refuse is still decided by `proc.stdout.strip()`, so a broken arm would only change the wording shown to the owner. A staged rename inside the rules folder would pin the arm.", "files": ["plugin/lib/learnings_compact.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Fixture-control comment says 'all but the two plain names' while asserting three unquoted", "recommendation": "In `test_every_name_git_prints_decodes_to_the_name_on_disk`, three names are unquoted: `plain name.md`, ` leading.md` and `arrow -> inside.md`. The assertion's `len(HARD_NAMES) - 3` is correct. Nothing reads the comment and no edit is wanted.", "files": ["tests/test_git_path_names.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "diff_header_path's midpoint branch recomputes (len(rest) - 5) // 2 five times in one backslash-continued condition", "recommendation": "Compute it once with `n, odd = divmod(len(rest) - 5, 2)`, then test `rest.startswith('a/') and not odd and rest[2+n:5+n] == ' b/' and rest[2:2+n] == rest[5+n:]`. That reads as the `a/X b/X` shape the comment describes. Behaviour is unchanged.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gitstate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "record_lint._resolve_artifact's loop variable shadows its `name` parameter", "recommendation": "`for name in gitstate.git_path_lines(out)` rebinds the function's `name` argument. Nothing reads the argument after the loop, so behaviour is unaffected, but the next edit that does will read a git path. Rename the loop variable (for example `rel`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Stray blank line inside record_lint._added_lines' git argument list", "recommendation": "There is an empty line after `\"--dst-prefix=b/\",` in the middle of the run_git argument tuple. Delete it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "critic_mode._paths_since_tree now reaches gitstate._run_git_text, a private runner, across modules", "recommendation": "This replaces an earlier cross-module call to the private `_git_text`, and `change_fragments` and `pr_gate` already borrow `_run_git_text` the same way, so it is not new coupling. If the name-read entry point in the finding above is built, this caller moves onto it and the private reach goes away. Otherwise, consider making the untrimmed read-only runner public.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md, .prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md, .prawduct/owner-rulings.md; none clears a bar", "recommendation": "No edit wanted. I checked the change record's claims against the code: the one decoder in gitstate, -z in test-reference-verify, core.quotepath=false gone from all three sites (git grep finds none left in plugin/), the lint's four rules and their runners, strip=False at the trimmed reads, and the midpoint header read with pinned prefixes. All of them hold. In the plan, the 'The sites' row for record_lint._added_lines ('drops a quoted name on purpose today') and Chunk 02's 'AST scan of plugin/lib and plugin/bin' are superseded, and the mid-build Decisions record both changes. Neither is shipped, and following them leads no one into a wrong action.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The plan's design decisions are still marked 'inferred, mine, open for the owner at the boundary review'", "recommendation": "These are design decisions (decode rather than -z everywhere, no process-wide core.quotepath, worktree_records left raw, test-reference-verify on -z), not goals. Each one records its reason and can be vetoed, so they meet Goal 5's recorded-why bar. This boundary review is the point the plan names for the owner to confirm them. No ruling for them exists yet in owner-rulings.md, so the confirm-or-correct belongs with the PR's owner decision. Nothing to fix in code.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "An unreadable diff header still drops its file's record-lint findings without any signal", "recommendation": "When gitstate.diff_header_path returns None, record_lint._parse_diff drops the file silently. The record_lint manifest has an `unchecked` channel, and this drop never reaches it. That drop already existed before this change. The change narrows it to malformed quoting, which git's own output never produces, so this is unreachable in practice and I am not rating it. If a reader ever hits the drop, routing the file into `unchecked` would make the lost answer visible rather than a quiet zero.", "files": ["plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "parse_porcelain_line's docstring says git does not quote ' -> '; porcelain v1 quotes any name holding a space", "recommendation": "Prose only, and nothing reads it. git status --porcelain (v1) wraps a name containing a space in quotes, so an ordinary or rename-source name holding ' -> ' arrives quoted there. The 'one ambiguous form' the docstring describes does not come from status output. The same wording appears in the change record and in the HARD_NAMES comment in tests/test_git_path_names.py ('a space (never quoted)'), which is true of diff --name-only but not of porcelain. The code is correct either way: it splits only on a rename and decodes quoted tokens. No edit wanted unless one of these sentences is touched anyway, and then make the claim specific to each format.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "tests/test_git_path_names.py"], "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "git-quotepath", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "a05d8b8e5c8c7efc92893b57ad43a24284b3c738", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-quotepath.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0, "test-scope-conftest": 0, "test-scope-autouse": 0, "test-scope-init": 0, "test-clock-fake": 0, "te